514:For $S\In V(G)$, let $|G(S)|$ denote the number of edges of $G$ that contain $S$ (counted with multiplicities). If $|S|=r$, then $|G(S)|$ is called the \defn{multiplicity of $S$ in $G$}. We say that $G$ is \defn{$F$-divisible} if $Deg(F)_{|S|}$ divides $|G(S)|$ for all $S\In V(G)$ with $|S|\le r-1$. An $F$-decomposition of $G$ is a collection $\cF$ of copies of $F$ in $G$ such that every edge $e\in G$ is covered precisely once. (Thus if $S\In V(G)$ has size $r$, then there are precisely $|G(S)|$ copies of $F$ in $\cF$ in which $S$ forms an edge.)
